A||||2||fierosound||Other Plastic Cars||||10||
Z||000000||fierosound||02-13-2000||05:18 PM||||One of the biggest things you get negative comments about is the plastic body, which in fact is one of the best things about a Fiero IMO, beasue of their dest/rust resistance. They seem to think was unique, that it was "an experiment" that didn't work out.<P>To these people I point out the other plastic vehicles GM made:<P>Buick Reatta 2 seat sportscar<BR>Chev Lumina APV van<BR>Pontiac Transport van<BR>Oldsmobile Silohette van<BR>Saturn<BR>Corvette<P>The Saturn is heavily advertised as having rust/dent resistant polymer panels. And of course, the Corvette, also had a body not of metal, but fiberglass. GM sold alot of these "non-metal" bodied cars...<P>You'll also notice almost all kit cars, from AC Cobra replicas up to Hummer replicas are fibrelass or plastic composites as well.  Some expensive exotics have bodies of fiberglass, aluminium or carbon fibre.  I guess if the price is high, or the name is exotic, it doesn't matter if it's "plastic".<P><p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 02-13-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000001||stimpy||02-13-2000||07:24 PM||||You can add to the list the Chevy Camaro, Buick LeSabre, Ford Mustang... the list of cars that use plastic body panels goes on and on. The Fiero was a frontrunner of all kinds of production advancements that are commonplace nowadays.<BR>But still it gets kicked around...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||02-13-2000||10:48 PM||||Just so everyone knows one of the reasons GM stopped making the Fiero was to make the plastic vans. There were 3 machines used for the plastic panels. 2 were used for the vans the 3rd was taken by the original Fiero management. Who by the way started Saturn! Then when GM stopped making the vans out of the plastic, Saturn scooped them up and now you have the LS series. So in a round about way Saturn's are the 2nd generation cousin to the Fiero!||||reg||1
